---
import { FLOW, NOTION } from '@lib/compare/notion-projects-alternative';

const notionPoints = [
  {
    text: 'Tasks are items in a database. The team picks their properties and views, and each view can be a board, a timeline, a calendar or a chart.',
    href: NOTION.views,
    label: 'Views, filters and sorts',
  },
  {
    text: 'An ID property with a prefix gives tasks keys, and sub-items and dependencies are switched on per database.',
    href: NOTION.uniqueId,
    label: 'Unique ID',
  },
  {
    text: 'The sprints setup adds current, next and last sprints and a Complete sprint button.',
    href: NOTION.sprints,
    label: 'Sprints',
  },
  {
    text: 'Docs, wikis, Notion AI and Notion MCP sit in the same workspace as the tasks.',
    href: NOTION.projects,
    label: 'Notion Projects',
  },
];

const ourPoints = [
  `Every issue has a key such as ${FLOW.key}, a type, a priority, a status, story points, a sprint, an estimate and spent time. You do not have to set any of this up.`,
  'Links read from both ends, and a depends-on loop is refused before it is saved.',
  'Sprints are planned, active, then completed. The burndown is recorded every night, so editing an issue later does not rewrite the chart.',
  'Each project has its own private knowledge base. An issue key in an article links to the issue, and the issue links back.',
];
---

<section id="np-approach" class="border-t border-line bg-app" aria-labelledby="np-approach-title">
  <div class="wrap py-16 md:py-24">
    <div class="max-w-[720px]">
      <h2 id="np-approach-title" class="h2">
        Issues should behave like issues, even when the team also writes docs.
      </h2>
      <p class="prose mt-5">
        A general workspace can be shaped into an issue tracker, and it can work well. The trade-off is who keeps the
        shape. In Notion Projects the team builds its own task model from databases and keeps it consistent. In Nothing
        Left the issue model comes built in. You cannot redesign it, and you do not have to maintain it.
      </p>
    </div>

    <div class="mt-10 md:mt-14 grid md:grid-cols-2 gap-6 lg:gap-8">
      <div class="panel">
        <h3 class="np-col-head">In Notion Projects, you assemble it</h3>
        <ul class="np-points">
          {notionPoints.map(point => (
            <li>
              {point.text}{' '}
              <a class="link font-normal np-src" href={point.href} rel="noopener">
                {point.label}
                <span class="arr ext" aria-hidden="true">
                  ↗
                </span>
              </a>
            </li>
          ))}
        </ul>
      </div>
      <div class="panel">
        <h3 class="np-col-head">In Nothing Left, it is already there</h3>
        <ul class="np-points">
          {ourPoints.map(point => (
            <li>{point}</li>
          ))}
        </ul>
      </div>
    </div>
    <p class="mt-5 text-xs text-fg-3 max-w-[680px]">
      Notion details are taken from its own product and help pages, linked above. A Notion workspace can be set up in
      many different ways, so we describe what it offers rather than draw one.
    </p>
  </div>
</section>

<style>
  .np-col-head {
    padding: 16px 20px;
    border-bottom: 1px solid var(--border);
    font-size: 15px;
    line-height: 22px;
    font-weight: 600;
  }
  .np-points {
    padding: 4px 20px 8px;
  }
  .np-points li {
    padding: 12px 0 12px 18px;
    position: relative;
    font-size: 14.5px;
    line-height: 22px;
    color: var(--text-secondary);
  }
  .np-points li + li {
    border-top: 1px solid var(--border-subtle);
  }
  .np-points li::before {
    content: '';
    position: absolute;
    left: 0;
    top: 20px;
    width: 6px;
    height: 6px;
    border-radius: 50%;
    background: var(--border-strong);
  }
  .np-src {
    font-size: 13px;
    white-space: nowrap;
  }
</style>
